¶
{{smaller block|When I was at Eton—now more than thirty years ago—the boys on the foundation were supplied in the dining-hall with an intermediate meal (if meal it could be called), which went under the name of ''beaver''. According to my recollection it consisted of beer only, and the hour was 4 P. M. {{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., II. 454.}}}}

'''bever<sup>3</sup>†''' (bē′vėr), ''v. i.'' [< ''bever''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.''] To take a bever or slight repast between meals.
{{smaller block|Your gallants never sup, breakfast, nor ''bever'' without me. {{right|''A. Brewer'' (?), Lingua, ii. 1.}}}}

'''beverage''' (bev′ẹ-rāj), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''beveridge'', ''beuvrage'', etc., < ME. ''beverage'', ''beveregе'', ''beverache'', ''beurage'', etc. (cf. ML. ''beveragium''), < OF. ''bevrage'', ''beuvrage'', ''breuvraige'', mod. F. ''breuvage'' (= Pr. ''beurage'' = Sp. ''bebrage'' = Pg. ''beberagem'' = It. ''beveraggio''; ML. as if *''biberaticum''), < ''bevre'', ''boivre'' = It. ''bevere'', < L. ''bibere'', drink: see ''bever''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.'', and ''-age''.] '''1.''' Drink of any kind; liquor for drinking: as, water is the common ''beverage''; intoxicating ''beverages''.
{{smaller block|<poem>A pleasant ''beverage'' he prepared before
Of wine and honey mixed.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., ii.}}}}

'''2.''' A name given specifically to various kinds of refreshing drinks. {{smaller|(''a'') In Devonshire, England, water-cider; a drink made by passing water through the crushed apples from which cider has been made. (''b'') A liquor made by passing water through the pressed grapes after the wine has been expressed.}}
{{smaller block|Touching price and quality of a liquor or drink called in England “''bevaradge''” and in France “pimpeene.” {{right|''Record Soc. Lancashire and Cheshire'', xi. 117.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') In the West Indies, a drink made of sugar-cane juice and water.}}

